\n\n$$ x = [0,1,0,0,0] $$\n\nAnd our embedding matrix:\n\n$$ W = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.2 & 0.4 & 0.1\\\\\\ 0.3 & 0.1 & 0.7\\\\\\ 0.9 & 0.2 & 0.3\\\\\\ 0.1 & 0.6 & 0.5\\\\\\ 0.4 & 0.8 & 0.1 \\end{bmatrix} $$\n\nThen:\n\n$$ xW = [0.3,0.1,0.7] $$\n\nNotice that this selects row 1.\n\nTherefore:\n\n> Embedding lookup is mathematically equivalent to multiplying a one-hot vector by the embedding matrix.\n\nHowever, PyTorch doesn't need to create the one-hot vector or perform the full matrix multiplication. It can directly retrieve the required row, which is much more efficient.\n\n## Parameters (Embedding)\n\nPyTorch provides additional options:\n\n```py\nnn.Embedding(\n    num_embeddings,\n    embedding_dim,\n    padding_idx=None,\n    max_norm=None,\n    norm_type=2.0,\n    scale_grad_by_freq=False,\n    sparse=False\n)\n```\n\nSome important ones:\n\n| Parameter            | Purpose                                                              |\n| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `padding_idx`        | Specifies a padding token whose embedding does not receive gradients |\n| `max_norm`           | Limits the norm of embedding vectors                                 |\n| `scale_grad_by_freq` | Scales gradients by inverse token frequency                          |\n| `sparse`             | Uses sparse gradients instead of dense gradients                     |","metadata":{}}]}